        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Both space flight and ground simulation of microgravity result in hemodynamic and biochemical changes in metabolites, enzymes and blood plasma hormones that correlate with deteriorated physical performance. Biochemical characterization of energy supply at rest does not provide full information on the body reserves, specifically the ability to maintain performance (i.e. during exercise). The study of the effects of graded exercise on these parameters during long-term bed rest has shown inadequate metabolic response to exercise and proved to be highly effective for assessment of applied countermeasures.</p>
<p>The primary objective of this experiment was to study directly the effect of long duration space flight on metabolic function during graded exercise on a bicycle ergometer, based on the levels of energy substrates and metabolites in the venous blood, enzyme activities and hormone levels regulating energy metabolism. This experiment was performed in conjunction with and to enhance another experiment entitled, “Aerobic Capacity using Graded Bicycle Ergometry” (3.2.1).</p>
<p>APPROACH:<br>
A baseline blood sample was to be taken 10 minutes prior to aerobic exercise by bicycle ergometry. Crewmembers had a light breakfast before the test and recorded in detail food and fluid intakes. Aerobic exercise was to be performed as detailed in Experiment 3.2.1. Immediately following exercise and after a 20 minute rest period, repeat blood samples were supposed to have been drawn.</p>
<p>Blood samples were obtained preflight and were used for hematocrit measurement and determination of lactic acid, pyruvic acid, lactate dehydrogenase activity (LDH), and lipid content. Enzyme activities of ATPase, LDH, and glyceraldehyde-6-phosphate dehydrogenase were determined on sample erythrocytes. For comparison purposes these parameters were determined on other days when exercise was not performed.</p>
<p>RESULTS:<br>
No results are available. This experiment was manifested on the Mir 18 mission. However, due to crew scheduling constraints, no data were obtained either preflight or inflight.</p>
<end>
        </div>

